id: pm-gh1643
title: "GH-1643: make pm claim --next --start one atomic select-claim-start write and type the claim-target refusals"
description: "https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1643\n\nReproduced on the current source build (2026.10.31) in a fresh non-git sandbox: `pm claim --next --start` exits 2 with the generic command_failed refusal 'Specify an item id or pass --next' and refusal.surface --next. Root cause: runClaimAction in src/cli/register-operations.ts routes any --start to requireClaimTarget(id, false), which ignores --next and emits the hint to pass the flag already passed. The MCP transport has the same defect: runMcpClaimAction (src/sdk/lifecycle/mcp-actions.ts) routes start to runMcpTaskCompositionAction, which requires an explicit id and refuses with 'Missing required argument: id'.\n\nSelecting, claiming and starting the next item is one agent intent. Today it takes claim --next followed by update --status in_progress: a crash between the two leaves a claimed-but-open item. claim --next already selects and claims under the item lock with an advancing race-loss walk (pm-114v, pm-fjxm). release --handoff already changes status inside the release mutation with enforceTypeWorkflowTransition (src/sdk/lifecycle/release-handoff.ts), so the same pattern gives an atomic claim-and-start.\n\nDuplicate search: 'claim next start', 'refusal suggests flag already provided', GH-1352, GH-1643; nearest are pm-114v and pm-fjxm (claim --next, closed), pm-gh1369 (claim --start compact receipts, closed), pm-ixi1 (provided flag listed as missing, closed) and pm-f05lsg (refusal closure gate, open)."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[4]: agent-ux,claim,github,refusal
created_at: "2026-10-31T21:13:57.945Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-01T02:39:19.500Z"
closed_at: "2026-10-31T22:31:19.294Z"
completed_at: "2026-10-31T22:31:19.294Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 180
acceptance_criteria: "pm claim --next --start selects, claims and moves the selected item to the workspace's in-progress status in one locked mutation with one history event, honours the selection filters, --if-available and the race-loss walk, enforces configured type workflows, and reports the start transition in the receipt; MCP pm_claim with next and start behaves identically; a claim target refusal never tells the caller to pass a flag already present and carries a typed code; regression tests cover CLI, MCP and the refusal hint"
parent: pm-mpbb
risk: medium
confidence: high
resolution: "runClaim accepts start and applyClaimStart moves the claimed item to the configured in-progress status inside the same mutateItem callback. That gives one claim history event, type-workflow enforcement, terminal_state_conflict for terminal items, and a start {from_status,to_status} receipt. runClaimNext forwards start through the race-loss walk. The CLI runClaimAction and MCP runMcpClaimAction route an id-less start to that path, while an explicit-id start keeps the claim-then-update composition and its typed refusal. requireClaimTarget now lives in the SDK claim module with missing_required_argument and ambiguous_option_combination codes and never names a flag the caller already passed. PmClient.claimNext accepts start. The MCP schema drops the start+next exclusion, and start+ifAvailable is exclusive only with an id."
expected_result: "pm claim --next --start claims and starts the next available item in one write on every transport, and no claim-target refusal recommends a flag the caller supplied."
actual_result: "Sandbox: pm claim --next --start returns status in_progress with changed_field_count 3 and history create then claim. MCP claim {next,start} and PmClient.claimNext({start:true}) return in_progress. pm claim --start refuses with missing_required_argument, suggesting pm claim --next --start. claim-start spec 7/7, register-commands 91/91, and lifecycle integration specs pass. With the routing stashed, the CLI/MCP start test fails. Full suite 11,748 passed."
