comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-31T21:46:58.955Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation: runClaim gained an optional start that calls applyClaimStart inside the same mutateItem callback (the release --handoff pattern). It resolves the in-progress status through resolveStartTaskInProgressStatus, refuses a terminal item with terminal_state_conflict even under --force, enforces type workflows via enforceTypeWorkflowTransition, and returns start {from_status,to_status}. runClaimNext forwards start to every candidate in the race-loss walk, so --if-available and filters behave as before. CLI runClaimAction and MCP runMcpClaimAction route start with an explicit id to the unchanged two-step composition, which keeps its typed 'Remove --next and --if-available' refusal; start without an id uses the atomic path or the new requireClaimTarget in src/sdk/lifecycle/claim-target.ts (missing_required_argument / ambiguous_option_combination, attributed to claim through the catalog generator's module map). runStartTask now strips start before its claim step so explicit --start stays claim-then-update. MCP schema: the start+next exclusion is gone and start+ifAvailable is exclusive only with an id. PmClient.claimNext({start:true}) is typed. Verified in a sandbox: claim --next --start → status in_progress, changed_field_count 3, history create→claim (one event)."
  "2026-10-31T23:02:07.900Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review round 1 on PR #1645. (1) The compact claim receipt now keeps the start {from_status,to_status} object (mutation-projection copies plain-object start), so default CLI JSON and MCP responses show the transition. (2) Major, predates this PR: resolveStartTaskInProgressStatus ignored a configured in_progress_status. A workflow declaring in_progress_status 'working' started items into the open status. It now returns the registry's in-progress status when it differs from the open status, and otherwise keeps the in_progress alias lookup (the 'building' alias workflow still resolves). Registration tests cover both. (3) Semantic attribution after the claim commit is best-effort: a failure returns the warning claim_semantic_attribution_unrecorded instead of an error for persisted ownership (claim-attribution spec). (4) MCP claim {id, next, start} was already refused with invalid_argument_value by the explicit-start composition; a test now pins it. (5) Starting an item with an open blocker is out of scope and matches update semantics; filed as pm-urzubi."
  "2026-11-01T01:17:43.802Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review round 2 on PR #1645: runMcpClaimAction now reads next only from the normalized runner options, so nested next:false overrides a flat next:true for both dispatch and the target check. The spec asserts {next:true, options:{next:false,start:true}} is refused with missing_required_argument instead of claiming an item."
  "2026-11-01T02:03:51.160Z","harness:claude-code","cubic review on PR #1645. Fixed: a quoted empty id (pm claim '' --start) now reaches the typed missing_required_argument refusal on CLI and MCP instead of the explicit composition. resolveStartTaskInProgressStatus never returns a configured in-progress status that the registry no longer defines (pm schema remove-status leaves the workflow reference); it falls back to the alias lookup and open status. The runClaim start option's docstring states it is the single-write variant used by ranked selection, while explicit --start keeps the composition. LIFECYCLE_COMMANDS now names the invalid_argument_value refusal for --start with --next or --if-available. Kept as is: MCP id precedence (flat id first) matches every lifecycle action's shared item-id resolution."
  "2026-11-01T02:39:19.500Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review round 3 on PR #1645: the docs now say ranking happens before any lock, and each candidate is re-read under its item lock before the single claim-and-start write. The selection itself was never atomic."
